          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What follow-up interval does guidance describe after starting a new blood pressure medicine?",
          "answer": "Guidance on the follow-up interval after starting a new blood pressure medication indicates that patients should have a monthly review until their target blood pressure is reached. This follow-up should include checks on medication adherence and kidney function, especially if the medication acts on the renin-angiotensin system. \n\nOnce blood pressure is stable and at the target level, follow-up can shift to every three to six months. This guidance is noted in the *Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line* document from major cardiology society consensus guidance. \n\nIt's important to discuss any concerns or specific situations with a healthcare clinician.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
